[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Data Availability Layers: Celestia, EigenDA, and EIP-4844 Blobs"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Data Availability (DA) is the foundational bottleneck of modular blockchain architectures. In traditional monolithic blockchains, a single network handles execution, consensus, and data availability. Modular blockchains decouple these functions, allowing specialized layers to optimize for specific tasks. This technical tutorial explores the architecture, mechanisms, and implementation paradigms of three leading DA solutions: Celestia, EigenDA, and Ethereum's EIP-4844 (Proto-Danksharding)."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"1. Understanding the Data Availability Problem"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"Before diving into specific solutions, we must define the Data Availability problem. When a block producer proposes a new block, they must make all transaction data available to the network. If the producer withholds a portion of the data, full nodes cannot verify the state transition, leading to potential security vulnerabilities such as invalid state transitions passing validation."},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Monolithic Bottleneck: All nodes download all data, limiting throughput to the capacity of the weakest node."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Light Client Vulnerability: Light clients typically download only block headers, meaning they trust that data is available unless cryptographic proofs state otherwise."},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Data Availability Sampling (DAS): Advanced cryptographic techniques allowing light clients to verify data availability without downloading entire blocks."},{"order":8,"type":"subheading","text":"2. Celestia: Sovereign and Modular DA via DAS"},{"order":9,"type":"paragraph","text":"Celestia is a dedicated modular consensus and data availability network. It does not execute smart contracts; instead, it orders transactions and guarantees that their data is available. It achieves this using 2D Reed-Solomon erasure coding and Data Availability Sampling."},{"order":10,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Erasure Coding: Original transaction data is expanded into a 2D matrix where data is redundantly encoded, allowing recovery if up to 50 percent of the chunks are missing."},{"order":11,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Namespaced Merkle Trees (NMTs): Celestia organizes block data into namespaces so rollups only need to download data relevant to their specific application."},{"order":12,"type":"code","text":"// Conceptual NMT validation logic in Go
